      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "721c17b8-891a-5aa8-81e5-901c21b4cfea",
        ":question/problem": "The derivative of $y=x-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)={sec}^{2}\u2061x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x) \\\\  & =1-{sec}^{2}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1-{sec}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
